---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
---

<Base title="ASCII FX — performance" active="performance">
  <h1>Performance</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Numbers, not adjectives. Everything below regenerates from <code>apps/benchmarks</code>; figures here were
    measured on an Apple-silicon laptop (Node 24 / Chrome headless WebGPU) — treat them as one representative
    machine, not a promise.
  </p>

  <h2>Warm render-loop guarantees</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>zero GPU→CPU readback (readback exists only in the explicit <code>captureFrame()</code>)</li>
    <li>zero per-cell JS allocations; buffers and textures are reused, reallocating only on grid changes</li>
    <li>zero runtime font work — the compiled atlas is uploaded once</li>
    <li>one fullscreen composite draw; pointer/time updates touch two small uniform buffers</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>GPU (WebGPU, exact matcher)</h2>
  <p>
    Full re-match of a 1280×720 source at 160×42 cells, full color, <i>including</i> source re-upload and a
    synchronous readback for verification: <b>~2.5–3.5ms</b> per frame in headless Chromium. The render-loop cost
    without readback is a fraction of that; pointer-only updates skip matching entirely.
  </p>

  <h2>Versus other libraries</h2>
  <p>
    Real published packages plus the techniques this project credits as influences, all fed the identical
    animated 1280×720 source at (or as near as each allows) the same 160×42 glyph grid. 200 timed frames after
    40 warmup, vsync disabled, each row in a fresh page (no cross-library GC or GPU contamination), best of two
    passes. Times are main-thread wall clock per frame and include drawing the source scene; the baseline row
    is that shared floor, so "over floor" is what each approach itself costs the main thread.
    <code>pnpm --filter @ascii-fx-internal/benchmarks compare</code> regenerates this.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>approach</th><th>picks glyphs by</th><th>grid</th><th class="num">p50 ms</th><th class="num">p95 ms</th><th class="num">over floor</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>baseline · scene only, no ascii</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td class="num">2.6</td><td class="num">3.0</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>ascii-fx · WebGPU</b></td><td><b>shape + fitted color (exact)</b></td><td>160×42</td><td class="num">2.7</td><td class="num">3.2</td><td class="num">+0.1</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ramp reference · mono*</td><td>brightness</td><td>160×42</td><td class="num">2.8</td><td class="num">3.1</td><td class="num">+0.2</td></tr>
      <tr><td>p5.asciify 0.10 (WebGL)</td><td>brightness + color</td><td>106×60</td><td class="num">3.0</td><td class="num">3.3</td><td class="num">+0.4</td></tr>
      <tr><td>three.js AsciiEffect (0.185)</td><td>brightness</td><td>160×42</td><td class="num">5.9</td><td class="num">6.4</td><td class="num">+3.3</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ramp reference · color*</td><td>brightness + color</td><td>160×42</td><td class="num">8.0</td><td class="num">8.5</td><td class="num">+5.4</td></tr>
      <tr><td>aalib.js 2.0 · mono</td><td>brightness</td><td>160×42</td><td class="num">9.5</td><td class="num">19.0</td><td class="num">+6.9</td></tr>
      <tr><td>aalib.js 2.0 · colored</td><td>brightness + color</td><td>160×42</td><td class="num">15.0</td><td class="num">34.3</td><td class="num">+12.4</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ascii-fx ramp matcher†</td><td>brightness (our cheapest opt-in)</td><td>160×42</td><td class="num">36.6</td><td class="num">39.7</td><td class="num">+34.0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ascii-fx shape6-lut (Harri-style)†</td><td>6-D shape vector + color</td><td>160×42</td><td class="num">37.7</td><td class="num">39.9</td><td class="num">+35.1</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ascii-fx · CPU fallback†</td><td>shape + fitted color (exact)</td><td>160×42</td><td class="num">38.8</td><td class="num">40.8</td><td class="num">+36.2</td></tr>
      <tr><td>chafa-wasm 0.3</td><td>shape-aware blocks + fg/bg</td><td>160×42</td><td class="num">45.3</td><td class="num">50.2</td><td class="num">+42.7</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note">
    The floor reframes the fast rows: ascii-fx WebGPU adds ~0.1ms of main-thread time — matching and
    compositing overlap on the GPU — while doing exact structural matching with per-cell color fitting; the
    brightness approaches (ramp reference, p5.asciify) sit just above it doing far less.
    * Not a library: the standard brightness-ramp technique hand-optimized with zero overhead — the floor any
    ramp library could reach; the gap up to aalib.js or AsciiEffect is that library's own cost.
    † Main-thread JS rows are composite-bound at this glyph size: matching alone is ~9.7ms for shape6-lut and
    ~9.4ms for ramp vs ~32ms for exact structural — the rest is the reference compositor. shape6 is the in-repo,
    independently-implemented version of Alex Harri's shape-vector approach, which this project credits as an
    influence alongside the Ditherlab/chafa structural-reconstruction lineage — represented here by chafa-wasm,
    its strongest living implementation (no separately runnable Ditherlab artifact exists to bench). Those are
    the only other shape-aware entrants, and both credited influences are in the table. The CPU rows exist for
    machines without WebGPU — the fallback costs speed, never quality.
  </p>

  <h2>CPU reference (single-threaded)</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>grid</th><th>color</th><th class="num">cells</th><th class="num">p50 ms</th><th class="num">cells/ms</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>80×21</td><td>full</td><td class="num">1,680</td><td class="num">10.0</td><td class="num">169</td></tr>
      <tr><td>120×32</td><td>full</td><td class="num">3,840</td><td class="num">19.6</td><td class="num">195</td></tr>
      <tr><td>160×42</td><td>full</td><td class="num">6,720</td><td class="num">32.3</td><td class="num">208</td></tr>
      <tr><td>240×63</td><td>full</td><td class="num">15,120</td><td class="num">68.8</td><td class="num">220</td></tr>
      <tr><td>320×84</td><td>full</td><td class="num">26,880</td><td class="num">118.3</td><td class="num">227</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note">
    Practical read: on machines without WebGPU, static images are a one-time ~30ms; live video is comfortable up
    to ~120 columns. The CPU path is the exact reference — the fallback costs speed, never quality.
  </p>

  <h2>Approximate matchers (opt-in)</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>matcher</th><th class="num">speed vs exact</th><th>quality</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>shape6 + LUT</td><td class="num">3.6×</td><td>small error deltas in full color; structural recall collapses on dense texture (worst corpus case 0.3%)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>shape6 brute</td><td class="num">2.5×</td><td>similar; no 512KiB LUT payload</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ramp</td><td class="num">3.9×</td><td>brightness only — an effect, not a fidelity mode</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Full per-image recall and reconstruction-error tables: <code>apps/benchmarks/RESULTS.md</code>. Selecting an
    approximate matcher is always explicit (<code>matcher: 'shape6'</code>); <code>auto</code> never downgrades.
  </p>

  <h2>Startup</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>compiled profile: ~13KB brotli, decoded with zero font work; pipelines compile async before first paint</li>
    <li>precompiled <code>.asciif</code> frames skip matching entirely at startup (~22KB brotli at 160 columns, full color)</li>
    <li>runtime <code>monospace</code> profiles exist for zero-config use — slower start, browser-dependent rasterization</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
